  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nThe sun is out this afternoon for most places except the tip of\nthe Arrowhead and northwest Wisconsin who are still dealing with\nremnant cloud cover from the warm air advection ahead of a\npassing cold front. A drier air mass will follow tonight into\nTuesday with high pressure passing by to our south. Expect quiet\nweather and another mild day on Tuesday with highs in the 50s.\n\nA quick clipper with a mesoscale rain band is still looking\nlikely to pass through Tuesday night into early Wednesday\nmorning. Not expecting much for impacts from this as\ntemperatures should be warm enough to keep this all rain. There\nis a possibility that the exact track of the rain could change\na little north or south, but right now it's roughly targeting\nareas along the US-2 corridor. \n\nFollowing this rain band, another cold front will pass through,\nand this one will pack a bit more of a punch. This will bring\nsome 850 hPa temperatures down to around -10C, and with the lake\nstill 15-20C warmer than that, lake-effect snow is expected\nalong the South Shore Wednesday night into Thursday. Winds will\nbe north to north-northeasterly, so in addition to the usual\nIron County suspects, parts of Ashland and eastern Bayfield\ncounties might be seeing some snow accumulations as well. SLRs\nprobably won't be too high with this, which may negate\naccumulations some, but lake-effect can often overachieve. The\nmost likely period for accumulating snow will be Wednesday\nevening through Thursday morning when temperatures will fall\nbelow freezing. GFS/ECMWF ensembles are variable in QPF amounts,\nbut many around 0.2-0.3\". Depending on what SLRs end up being,\nat minimum we could see 1-2\" of snow, but can't rule out some\nlocally higher amounts around 3\". Most likely locations will be\nin the higher terrain of Ashland and Iron counties. Once\ntemperatures rise above freezing later Thursday morning,\nlingering light snow or rain showers will be possible, but it\nshould start to melt on contact at that point. Lingering light\nsnow is possible Thursday night, mainly for Iron county, and a\npassing trough aloft could enhance that slightly. Drier air will\nalso be moving in with flow becoming a bit closer to north-\nnorthwesterly, so accumulations should be very minimal. \n\nElsewhere, look for dry/quiet weather with highs in the 30s\nThursday/Friday. As we go into the weekend, ridging over the\nwestern CONUS will move east with southerly flow developing. As\nof now, conditions are looking quiet with dry weather expected.\nTemperatures will start warming up with highs in the 40s by\nSunday. We may remain in a pretty quiet dry weather pattern\nuntil sometime next week after the upper ridge passes by and we\nget into a pattern more favorable for Colorado lows on the back\nside.",
